         <td style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 10pt;" background="/images/bg_heading_green_01.jpg"><center><b>Dreams of Magic
: The Story of Enhasa
 by Rycar of Prexus, Paladin of Life
</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
		 <td>                            
	                                   

	He had told me once that knowledge and magic were born of dreams, and he believed Erudin was where dreams came true. You see, he had never left the city and its neighboring forest. He wished to always live in his dreams. That is, until 10 days before I arrived. <br><br>	He became more excited by the second as he told me of his plan.  He had found pieces of a tome. A tome that could, in theory,  contain the greatest secret ever created. As he spoke of the secret, I could sense a faint trace of unease in his voice, and to this day I wonder if he had an inkling of what was to come....<br><br>	Apparently the Library had collected many of the pages, but many pages still were lost to the ages. He spoke of a great Library, a hidden one, located beneath the Frozen Plains, the library of a long dead madman named Miragul. His story, however, has been told many times over, and I feel no need to repeat it here.  Enhasa had learned of a portal from the lost necromancer city of Paineel to Miragul's laboratory. <br><br>"Necromancers?!  Are you insane? You'll never survive!"<br><br>"Knowledge is the greatest quest. You have chosen the path of almost certain death in your quests, as I well know from hearing your endless stories.  Why cannot I risk my life in mine?"<br><br>There was nothing I could say.<br><br>"And though I loathe the forest, I must pass through it on my way. Accompany me or don't Paladin. I'm doing this."<br><br><br>Even Tull was more agreeable than he. <br><br>	He slept at the inn that night, as by the time we'd finished talking the palace was closed. I had a hard time falling asleep that night, but Enhasa slept just fine. Dreaming dreams of magic I supposed.<br><br>	The next day we left the magic city, for the forest of Toxxulia. Tox forest resembled a black sheet of paper, its darkness rivaling that of Nektulos or Kithicor Woods. Enhasa, however, seemed to know the path to his destination, following the invisible road like a rabid wolf on its last legs. Suddenly in the distance I saw a light, which quickly revealed itself to be a torch upon a marble entry way into a cave. I sensed the horrible presence of the undead as I approached, and quickly dispatched of the two skeleton guards within the maw.  Enhasa revealed to me that he wished to enter alone, and I complied, albiet unwillingly. He approached the entrance and turned again to face me. Then upon his face appeared a smile, a knowing smile. That was the first and last time I ever saw him smile. <br><br>	The Child of Magic stepped into the mouth of the cave, the dark wind coming from it blowing his white cloak around him, creating for him an etherial presence. Walking slowly at first, and then at a faster pace, I was unsure if he was going to continue in his pursuit. I soon found that was not something I had to concern myself with.<br><br>	He walked into the cave, but was thrown backwards. A barrier of magic was put up a little ways into the cave. Being a man of magic himself, running into the barrier took a bit out of him. Yet, he tried again, and again, until I saw he  could take no more. Despite this, he wearily approached the barrier once again. I yelled out for him to stop, but even if he did hear me, he did not obey. There was a short crack, and I saw his lifeless body appear before me at the entrance.  Without a thought I called upon Rodcet Nife to grant me the power to Lay of Hands, but even this was not enough to revive Enhasa completely. It would, however, keep him alive until I could take him to the Clerics of Erudin.<br><br>	I'll bet the Clerics were a bit suspect when they saw me appear in the doorway, Enhasa in my hands.  They took him to a higher level, and told me to go to the bar to wait. I drowned my worry for about half an hour before I decided to check on the Dreamer. He was in the bed they had prepared for him, with most of the clerics and paladins gathered around. Enhasa appeared to once again be dreaming his magic dreams. Before the head cleric could utter a word, Enhasa rose from his bed.<br><br>	There was something different about him when he rose, although that is an understatement. The etherial quality from earlier remained, yet it seemed somehow darker. He uttered an incantation that sent shivers up my spine, and disappeared. 10 minutes later, Felwilthe exploded.<br><br><br>	That was five years ago. Many of the survivors of Felwilthe's explosion claimed to have seen a Erudite garbed in white before the explosion. (Of course, many of Felwilthe's survivors also claim that Norrath is, in actuality, a giant elephant.) Tull and I have spent a good part of the the last five years trying to find out what happened to Enhasa, and Felwilthe. We are on our way to Felwilthe now, by boat, to investigate about a discovery that was made there 10 days ago, and has been stolen. I stand by the mast of this boat as I write this. And it looks like its about to rain.         <br>
